/** @page fast_text_processing Fast Text Processing

    @tableofcontents

    @section ftp_overview Overview

    %Qore 3.0 introduces a coordinated set of zero-allocation text-scanning primitives aimed at
    tokenizers, parsers, log scrubbers, and any other code that traverses large strings on a hot
    path.  Three layers cooperate:

    1. <b>Internal string views</b> — read-only @ref string_views "string view" infrastructure
       lets @ref <string>::substr() "<string>::substr()" and the
       @ref string_slicing "[] slice operator" share the original buffer instead of copying.
    2. <b>Allocation-free byte/codepoint access</b> — the
       @ref <string>::getByte() "<string>::getByte()" pseudo-method and the
       @ref Qore::Scanner "Scanner" class expose cursor-based reads that never allocate
       per-character.
    3. <b>Lazy iterators</b> — @ref Qore::StringIterator "StringIterator",
       @ref Qore::StringCharIterator "StringCharIterator",
       @ref Qore::StringSplitIterator "StringSplitIterator", and
       @ref Qore::StringRegexSplitIterator "StringRegexSplitIterator" yield codepoints,
       delimited tokens, and regex-delimited lines on demand, replacing eager
       @ref <string>::split() "<string>::split()" / list-returning patterns where memory or
       latency matters.

    These APIs sit alongside the older @ref <string>::split() "<string>::split()",
    @ref <string>::substr() "<string>::substr()", and list-returning regex helpers.  String
    iteration now has direct language support through @ref char "char" values:
    @ref <string>::iterator() "<string>::iterator()" and <tt>iterate string</tt>
    yield chars, while @ref <string>::codePointIterator() "<string>::codePointIterator()" remains
    available for integer codepoints.

    @section ftp_byte_access Byte- and Codepoint-Indexed Access

    @par <string>::getByte()
    @parblock
    Returns the byte value (\c 0-255) at the given byte offset, or @ref nothing if the offset is
    out of range.  No allocation, no copy, no encoding conversion.  Negative offsets count from
    the end of the string.

    @code{.py}
string s = "abc";
*int b = s.getByte(0);  # 97 (ASCII 'a')
*int e = s.getByte(-1); # 99 (ASCII 'c')
*int x = s.getByte(99); # NOTHING
    @endcode

    Use @ref <string>::getByte() "getByte()" as the primitive in tight scan loops where the
    input is known to be ASCII or where you only care about specific byte values (e.g.
    delimiters, escape characters, ASCII control bytes).  For codepoint-aware reads use the
    @ref Qore::Scanner "Scanner" class or a @ref Qore::StringIterator "StringIterator".
    @endparblock

    @par Scanner
    @parblock
    @ref Qore::Scanner "Scanner" wraps a string with a cursor exposing byte-level and
    codepoint-level peek/advance/match operations.  Construction is allocation-free for the
    string buffer (the source is shared via the view layer); the only allocations during scanning
    are explicit substring captures.

    @code{.py}
Scanner sc("key=value;next=other");
string key = sc.captureUntil("=");
sc.advance();                          # consume '='
string value = sc.captureUntil(";");
sc.advance();                          # consume ';'
    @endcode

    Typical applications include hand-written tokenizers (@ref qdxintro "Qdx",
    @ref dataproviderintro "DPQL", JSON-like protocols), URL and header parsers, log-line
    scrubbers, and any place where a regex would be over-powered for the matching but a
    per-character function-call loop would be too slow.
    @endparblock

    @section ftp_iterators Lazy Iterators

    The lazy text iterators all return immediately and produce one element at a time when
    iterated, with constant memory regardless of input size.

    @par <string>::iterator() / StringIterator
    Iterate codepoints (not bytes) without materialising a list.  Each element is a @ref char value.
    This is also the iterator used by <tt>iterate</tt> for string sources.

    @par <string>::codePointIterator() / StringCharIterator
    Iterate codepoints as integers without materialising a list.  This preserves the pre-char API for
    callers that need numeric Unicode codepoint values directly.

    @par <string>::splitString() / StringSplitIterator
    Lazy delimiter split.  Equivalent to @ref <string>::split() "<string>::split()" but without
    building the full list up front — useful for very large inputs or when the consumer can
    short-circuit.

    @par <string>::splitChars() / StringSplitIterator
    Lazy per-codepoint split, yielding each codepoint as a single-character string.

    @par <string>::splitLines() / StringSplitIterator
    Lazy newline-delimited line split.

    @par <string>::splitLinesRegex() / StringRegexSplitIterator
    Lazy regex-delimited split.  Replaces the previous eager list-returning behaviour of the
    @ref <string>::splitLinesRegex() "<string>::splitLinesRegex()" pseudo-method.

    @par Iterator native fast-path dispatch
    @parblock
    The @ref map "map", @ref select "select", and
    @ref foldl "foldl" functional operators (and their hash/object analogues)
    detect these iterators and dispatch them through a native C++ fast path that bypasses
    @ref Qore::AbstractIterator "AbstractIterator" virtual calls per element.  Iterator
    subclasses written in %Qore retain the original dispatch path, so the fast path is opt-in
    by data type rather than a behavioural change.
    @endparblock

    @section ftp_when_to_use When to Use Which API

    | Goal | Recommended API |
    |------|-----------------|
    | Read a single byte at a known offset | @ref <string>::getByte() "<string>::getByte()" |
    | Hand-written tokenizer / parser | @ref Qore::Scanner "Scanner" |
    | Iterate characters as char values | @ref <string>::iterator() "<string>::iterator()" or <tt>iterate string</tt> |
    | Iterate codepoints as integers | @ref <string>::codePointIterator() "<string>::codePointIterator()" |
    | Lazy delimiter split (large input or short-circuit) | @ref <string>::splitString() "<string>::splitString()" |
    | Lazy line split | @ref <string>::splitLines() "<string>::splitLines()" |
    | Lazy regex split | @ref <string>::splitLinesRegex() "<string>::splitLinesRegex()" |
    | Format a binary as hex | @ref <binary>::toHexArray() "<binary>::toHexArray()" |
    | Existing eager full-split is fine | @ref <string>::split() "<string>::split()" (unchanged) |

    @section ftp_perf_notes Performance Notes

    The internal infrastructure that makes the new APIs fast also accelerates several existing
    operations on large strings:

    - @ref <string>::substr() "<string>::substr()" and the @ref string_slicing "[] slice operator"
      no longer copy the slice buffer when the result is consumed read-only — the result aliases
      the source until the first write.
    - The internal @ref Util::stripAnsi() "Util::stripAnsi()" helper, the
      @ref Qdx::QoreTokenizer "Qdx::QoreTokenizer" lexical analyser, and the
      @ref dataproviderintro "DataProvider" DPQL tokenizer were all ported to byte-indexed
      cursor patterns and observed end-user speedups in the 4x–100x range depending on
      workload.

